WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.708 --> 00:00:13.976
[Oliver Jeffers]

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.833
[An ode to living on Earth]

00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:18.601
Hello.

00:00:18.625 --> 00:00:21.601
I'm sure by the time
I get to end of this sentence,

00:00:21.625 --> 00:00:23.101
given how I talk,

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:25.976
you'll all have figured out
that I'm from a place called

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.268
planet Earth.

00:00:27.292 --> 00:00:29.018
Earth is pretty great.

00:00:29.042 --> 00:00:30.875
It's home to us.

00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:33.393
And germs.

00:00:33.417 --> 00:00:35.726
Those [blip] take a back seat
for the time being,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.726
because believe it or not,
they're not the only thing going on.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.393
This planet is also home
to cars, brussels sprouts;

00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:44.018
those weird fish things
that have their own headlights;

00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:45.518
art, fire,

00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:46.809
fire extinguishers,

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:48.934
laws, pigeons, bottles of beer,

00:00:48.958 --> 00:00:50.226
lemons and light bulbs;

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.893
Pinot noir and paracetamol;

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.476
ghosts, mosquitoes, flamingos, flowers,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.059
the ukulele, elevators and cats,

00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:57.559
cat videos, the internet;

00:00:57.583 --> 00:00:59.351
iron beams, buildings and batteries,

00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:01.851
all ingenuity and bright ideas,
all known life ...

00:01:01.875 --> 00:01:03.518
and a whole bunch of other stuff.

00:01:03.542 --> 00:01:06.059
Pretty much everything we know
and ever heard of.

00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:08.059
It's my favorite place, actually.

00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:09.643
This small orb,

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:12.208
floating in a cold and lonely
part of the cosmos.

00:01:13.375 --> 00:01:16.434
Oh, the accent is from Belfast,
by the way, which is ...

00:01:16.458 --> 00:01:17.726
here.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.000
Roughly.

00:01:19.875 --> 00:01:22.226
You may think you know this planet Earth,

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.518
as you're from here.

00:01:23.542 --> 00:01:24.809
But chances are,

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.500
you probably haven't thought
about the basics in a while.

00:01:27.524 --> 00:01:28.934
I thought I knew it.

00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:30.393
Thought I was an expert, even.

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:33.351
Until, that is, I had to explain
the entire place,

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:34.851
and how it's supposed to work,

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:37.518
to someone who had never been here before.

00:01:37.542 --> 00:01:38.809
Not what you might think,

00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:41.018
although my dad always did say

00:01:41.042 --> 00:01:43.059
the sure sign of intelligent
life out there

00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:45.476
is that they haven't bothered
trying to contact us.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.143
It was actually my newborn son
I was trying to explain things to.

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:50.809
We'd never been parents before,

00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:52.101
my wife and I,

00:01:52.125 --> 00:01:55.726
and so treated him like most guests
when he arrived home for the first time,

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.018
by giving him the tour.

00:01:57.042 --> 00:01:58.643
This is where you live, son.

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:00.351
This room is where we make food at.

00:02:00.375 --> 00:02:03.684
This is the room we keep
our collection of chairs, and so on.

00:02:03.708 --> 00:02:04.976
It's refreshing,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.684
explaining how our planet works
to a zero-year-old.

00:02:07.708 --> 00:02:08.976
But after the laughs,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.184
and once the magnitude that new humans
know absolutely nothing

00:02:12.208 --> 00:02:15.226
settles on you and how little
you know either,

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.393
explaining the whole planet
becomes quite intimidating.

00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:19.809
But I tried anyway.

00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:22.893
As I walked around those first few weeks,

00:02:22.917 --> 00:02:25.226
narrating the world as I saw it,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.393
I began to take notes
of the ridiculous things I was saying.

00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:30.726
The notes slowly morphed into a letter

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.059
intended for my son
once he learned to read.

00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:34.559
And that letter became a book

00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:37.268
about the basic principles
of what it is to be a human

00:02:37.292 --> 00:02:40.226
living on Earth in the 21st century.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.726
Some things are really obvious.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.684
Like, the planet is made of two parts:

00:02:44.708 --> 00:02:47.101
land and sea.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:50.393
Some less obvious
until you think about them.

00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:52.184
Like, time.

00:02:52.208 --> 00:02:55.208
Things can sometimes
move slowly here on Earth.

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:58.601
But more often, they move quickly.

00:02:58.625 --> 00:03:01.375
So use your time well,
it will be gone before you know it.

00:03:02.417 --> 00:03:03.976
Or people.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.559
People come in all different
shapes, sizes and colors.

00:03:07.583 --> 00:03:08.976
We may all look different,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.101
act different and sound different,

00:03:11.125 --> 00:03:12.393
but don't be fooled.

00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:13.684
We are all people.

00:03:13.708 --> 00:03:16.934
It doesn't skip me that of all
the places in the universe,

00:03:16.958 --> 00:03:18.226
people only live on Earth,

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.851
can only live on Earth.

00:03:19.875 --> 00:03:22.059
And even then,
only on some of the dry bits.

00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:24.934
There's only a very small
part of the surface of our planet

00:03:24.958 --> 00:03:26.934
that is actually habitable to human life,

00:03:26.958 --> 00:03:29.309
and squeezed in here
is where all of us live.

00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:32.143
It's easy to forget
when you're up close to the dirt,

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:34.518
the rocks, the foliage,
the concrete of our lands,

00:03:34.542 --> 00:03:37.684
just how limited the room
for maneuvering is.

00:03:37.708 --> 00:03:40.143
From a set of eyes close to the ground,

00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:42.684
the horizon feels like it goes forever.

00:03:42.708 --> 00:03:44.601
After all, it's not an every-day ritual

00:03:44.625 --> 00:03:47.018
to consider where we are
on the ball of our planet

00:03:47.042 --> 00:03:49.018
and where that ball is in space.

00:03:49.042 --> 00:03:51.976
I didn't want to tell my son
the same story of countries

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.893
that we were told where I was growing up
in Northern Ireland.

00:03:54.917 --> 00:03:56.768
That we were from just a small parish,

00:03:56.792 --> 00:03:59.143
which ignores life
outside its immediate concerns.

00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:02.809
I wanted to try to feel
what it was like to see our planet

00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:05.184
as one system, as a single object,

00:04:05.208 --> 00:04:06.643
hanging in space.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:07.934
To do this,

00:04:07.958 --> 00:04:10.434
I would need to switch
from flat drawings for books

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:11.976
to 3D sculpture for the street,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.809
and I'd need almost 200 feet,

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:15.268
a New York City block,

00:04:15.292 --> 00:04:17.559
to build a large-scale model of the moon,

00:04:17.583 --> 00:04:19.809
the Earth and us.

00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:23.024
This project managed to take place
on New York City's High Line park

00:04:23.048 --> 00:04:24.268
last winter,

00:04:24.292 --> 00:04:27.309
on the 50th anniversary
of Apollo 11's mission around the Moon.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.684
After its installation,

00:04:28.708 --> 00:04:31.226
I was able to put on
a space helmet with my son

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.184
and launch, like Apollo 11 did
half a century ago,

00:04:34.208 --> 00:04:36.018
towards the Moon.

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:37.309
We circled around

00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:39.809
and looked back at us.

00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:41.309
What I felt was

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:43.809
how lonely it was there in the dark.

00:04:43.833 --> 00:04:45.559
And I was just pretending.

00:04:45.583 --> 00:04:47.101
The Moon is the only object

00:04:47.125 --> 00:04:49.351
even remotely close to us.

00:04:49.375 --> 00:04:51.268
And at the scale of this project,

00:04:51.292 --> 00:04:53.309
where our planet was 10 feet in diameter,

00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:55.893
Mars, the next planet,
will be the size of a yoga ball

00:04:55.917 --> 00:04:58.434
and a couple of miles away.

00:04:58.458 --> 00:05:00.643
Although borders
are not visible from space,

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:01.976
on my sculpture,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.572
every single border was drawn in.

00:05:03.596 --> 00:05:06.601
But rather than writing the country names
on the carved-up land,

00:05:06.625 --> 00:05:08.476
I wrote over and over again,

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.268
"people live here, people live here."

00:05:11.292 --> 00:05:13.684
"People live here."

00:05:13.708 --> 00:05:16.184
And off on the Moon, it was written,

00:05:16.208 --> 00:05:17.976
"No one lives here."

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.601
Often, the obvious things

00:05:19.625 --> 00:05:22.684
aren't all that obvious
until you think about them.

00:05:22.708 --> 00:05:25.184
Seeing anything
from a vast enough distance

00:05:25.208 --> 00:05:26.643
changes everything,

00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:28.893
as many astronauts have experienced.

00:05:28.917 --> 00:05:31.309
And human eyes
have only ever seen our Earth

00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:33.184
from as far as the Moon, really.

00:05:33.208 --> 00:05:34.476
It's quite a ways further

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.934
before we get to the edges
of our own Solar System.

00:05:36.958 --> 00:05:40.351
And even out to other stars,
to the constellations.

00:05:40.375 --> 00:05:43.518
There is actually only one point
in the entire cosmos

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:46.393
that is present in all
constellations of stars,

00:05:46.417 --> 00:05:48.684
and that presence is

00:05:48.708 --> 00:05:51.059
here, planet Earth.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:53.703
Those pictures we have made up
for the clusters of stars

00:05:53.727 --> 00:05:57.144
only make sense from
this point of view down here.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.684
Their stories only make sense
here on Earth.

00:06:00.708 --> 00:06:03.184
And only something to us.

00:06:03.208 --> 00:06:04.559
To people.

00:06:04.583 --> 00:06:07.268
We are creatures of stories.

00:06:07.292 --> 00:06:08.893
We are the stories we tell,

00:06:08.917 --> 00:06:11.143
we're the stories we're told.

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:15.018
Consider briefly the story
of human civilization on Earth.

00:06:15.042 --> 00:06:17.601
It tells of the ingenuity, elegance,

00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:19.934
generous and nurturing nature of a species

00:06:19.958 --> 00:06:22.768
that is also self-focused, vulnerable

00:06:22.792 --> 00:06:24.601
and defiantly protective.

00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:27.559
We, the people, shield
the flame of our existence

00:06:27.583 --> 00:06:30.268
from the raw, vast elements
outside our control,

00:06:30.292 --> 00:06:32.268
the great beyond.

00:06:32.292 --> 00:06:35.292
Yet it is always to the flame we look.

00:06:36.458 --> 00:06:38.059
"For all we know,"

00:06:38.083 --> 00:06:39.351
when said as a statement,

00:06:39.375 --> 00:06:42.143
it means the sum total of all knowledge.

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:43.601
But when said another way,

00:06:43.625 --> 00:06:45.559
"for all we know,"

00:06:45.583 --> 00:06:48.393
it means that we do not know at all.

00:06:48.417 --> 00:06:52.518
This is the beautiful,
fragile drama of civilization.

00:06:52.542 --> 00:06:54.851
We are the actors and spectators
of a cosmic play

00:06:54.875 --> 00:06:56.518
that means the world to us here,

00:06:56.542 --> 00:06:58.684
but means nothing anywhere else.

00:06:58.708 --> 00:07:00.976
Possibly not even that much
down here, either.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.684
If we truly thought about
our relationship with our boat,

00:07:03.708 --> 00:07:04.976
with our Earth,

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.875
it might be more of a story
of ignorance and greed.

00:07:09.208 --> 00:07:11.226
As is the case with Fausto,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.143
a man who believed he owned everything

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:15.726
and set out to survey what was his.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.101
He easily claims ownership of a flower,

00:07:18.125 --> 00:07:20.684
a sheep, a tree and a field.

00:07:20.708 --> 00:07:23.059
The lake and the mountain
prove harder to conquer,

00:07:23.083 --> 00:07:25.101
but they, too, surrender.

00:07:25.125 --> 00:07:27.309
It is in trying to own the open sea

00:07:27.333 --> 00:07:29.768
where his greed proves his undoing,

00:07:29.792 --> 00:07:31.851
when, in a fit of arrogance,

00:07:31.875 --> 00:07:34.851
he climbs overboard
to show that sea who is boss.

00:07:34.875 --> 00:07:36.601
But he does not understand,

00:07:36.625 --> 00:07:39.458
slips beneath the waves,
sinks to the bottom.

00:07:40.333 --> 00:07:41.601
The sea was sad for him

00:07:41.625 --> 00:07:43.750
but carried on being the sea.

00:07:44.917 --> 00:07:47.393
As do all the other objects
of his ownership,

00:07:47.417 --> 00:07:50.458
for the fate of Fausto
does not matter to them.

00:07:51.583 --> 00:07:55.351
For all the importance in the cosmos
we believe we hold,

00:07:55.375 --> 00:07:57.018
we'd have nothing

00:07:57.042 --> 00:07:58.601
if not for this Earth.

00:07:58.625 --> 00:08:01.059
While it would keep happily spinning,

00:08:01.083 --> 00:08:03.101
obliviously without us.

00:08:03.125 --> 00:08:05.375
On this planet, there are people.

00:08:06.292 --> 00:08:07.643
We have gone about our days,

00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:09.726
sometimes we look up and out,

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.893
mostly we look down and in.

00:08:12.917 --> 00:08:15.934
Looking up and by drawing lines
between the lights in the sky,

00:08:15.958 --> 00:08:18.726
we've attempted to make
sense out of chaos.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:22.268
Looking down, we've drawn lines
across the land to know where we belong

00:08:22.292 --> 00:08:23.559
and where we don't.

00:08:23.583 --> 00:08:26.393
We do mostly forget that these lines
that connect the stars

00:08:26.417 --> 00:08:28.184
and those lines that divide the land

00:08:28.208 --> 00:08:30.518
live only in our heads.

00:08:30.542 --> 00:08:33.018
They, too, are stories.

00:08:33.042 --> 00:08:35.393
We carry out our everyday
routines and rituals

00:08:35.417 --> 00:08:37.476
according to the stories
we most believe in,

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.977
and these days, the story
is changing as we write it.

00:08:40.001 --> 00:08:42.143
There is a lot of fear
in this current story,

00:08:42.167 --> 00:08:43.434
and until recently,

00:08:43.458 --> 00:08:45.643
the stories that seemed
to have the most power

00:08:45.667 --> 00:08:46.934
are those of bitterness,

00:08:46.958 --> 00:08:50.101
of how it had all gone wrong for us
individually and collectively.

00:08:50.125 --> 00:08:53.434
It has been inspiring to watch
how the best comes from the worst.

00:08:53.458 --> 00:08:56.226
How people are waking up
in this time of global reckoning

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.726
to the realization that our
connections with each other

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:01.893
are some of the most
important things we have.

00:09:01.917 --> 00:09:03.184
But stepping back.

00:09:03.208 --> 00:09:04.643
For all we've had to lament,

00:09:04.667 --> 00:09:07.684
we spend very little time relishing
the single biggest thing

00:09:07.708 --> 00:09:09.643
that has ever gone right for us.

00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:11.393
That we are here in the first place,

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:12.684
that we are alive at all.

00:09:12.708 --> 00:09:14.268
That we are still alive.

00:09:14.292 --> 00:09:17.226
A million and a half years
after finding a box of matches,

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:20.184
we haven't totally burned the house down.

00:09:20.208 --> 00:09:21.476
Yet.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.726
The chances of being here
are infinitesimal.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.018
Yet here we are.

00:09:25.042 --> 00:09:26.351
Perils and all.

00:09:26.375 --> 00:09:28.768
There have never been
more people living on Earth.

00:09:28.792 --> 00:09:30.226
Using more stuff.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:32.768
And it's become obvious
that many of the old systems

00:09:32.792 --> 00:09:34.726
we invented for ourselves

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.018
are obsolete.

00:09:36.042 --> 00:09:37.976
And we have to build new ones.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.434
If it wasn't germs,

00:09:39.458 --> 00:09:42.559
our collective fire
might suffocate us before long.

00:09:42.583 --> 00:09:45.643
As we watch the wheels
of industry grind to a halt,

00:09:45.667 --> 00:09:47.726
the machinery of progress become silent,

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:50.143
we have the wildest of opportunities

00:09:50.167 --> 00:09:52.059
to hit the reset button.

00:09:52.083 --> 00:09:53.934
To take a different path.

00:09:53.958 --> 00:09:56.434
Here we are on Earth.

00:09:56.458 --> 00:09:59.059
And life on Earth is a wonderful thing.

00:09:59.083 --> 00:10:00.351
It looks big, this Earth,

00:10:00.375 --> 00:10:02.226
but there are lots of us on here.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:04.809
Seven and a half billion at last count,

00:10:04.833 --> 00:10:07.601
with more showing up every day.

00:10:07.625 --> 00:10:08.893
Even so,

00:10:08.917 --> 00:10:10.643
there is still enough for everyone,

00:10:10.667 --> 00:10:12.000
if we all share a little.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.684
So please,

00:10:14.708 --> 00:10:15.958
be kind.

00:10:17.125 --> 00:10:18.768
When you think of it another way,

00:10:18.792 --> 00:10:21.018
if Earth is the only place
where people live,

00:10:21.042 --> 00:10:24.250
it's actually the least
lonely place in the universe.

00:10:25.375 --> 00:10:27.601
There are plenty of people to be loved by

00:10:27.625 --> 00:10:29.684
and plenty of people to love.

00:10:29.708 --> 00:10:31.809
We need each other.

00:10:31.833 --> 00:10:33.750
We know that now, more than ever.

00:10:34.958 --> 00:10:36.208
Good night.

